<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Jing of Han</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/188-bce">188</a>–<a href="/years/141-bce">141 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Liu Qi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Jing of Han, born Liu Qi, was the sixth emperor of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, ruling from 157 to 141 BCE. Early in his reign he suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-seven-states">Rebellion of the Seven States</a> of 154 BCE and then cut back the powers of the semi-autonomous kingdoms. His reign and his father Emperor Wen's are remembered as a period of light taxation and recovery, and he was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-jing-of-han.jpg" alt="Emperor Jing of Han" width="320" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wang Qi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SancaiJingdiHan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liu Qi was born in 188 BCE, the eldest son of Emperor Wen, then Prince of Dai, and Consort Dou. After his father became emperor in November 180 BCE he was made crown prince in February or March 179 BCE, and his mother became empress. His mother was a Taoist who required her children and grandchildren to study Taoist doctrine, and she deeply influenced him. A member of his household, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-cuo">Chao Cuo</a>, known for intelligence, ruthless efficiency and rhetorical talent, became a trusted adviser. Prince Qi had an epicurean lifestyle, and Emperor Wen once considered replacing him with his younger brother Liu Wu, Prince of Liang, but dropped the idea when many officials opposed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Emperor Wen died in July 157 BCE and Prince Qi became emperor. Many officials expected a poor ruler, but he proved capable. He continued his father's policy of non-interference with the people and cut taxes in half, to one thirtieth of the crop. He also reduced criminal penalties, and in 156 BCE cut the number of whip strokes after finding that his father's abolition of the mutilating punishments had caused more people to die from whipping. He kept the marriage treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, which largely avoided conflict on the northern frontier.</p><p>The princes of the imperial clan, who often built up their own armies and resisted edicts, remained a problem. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-cuo">Chao Cuo</a>'s advice Emperor Jing took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a> and counties from the principalities of Chu, Zhao, Jiaoxi and Wu in 154 BCE. Wu rebelled in alliance with Chu, Jiaoxi, Zhao and three smaller principalities, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-seven-states">Rebellion of the Seven States</a>, and Emperor Jing executed Chao Cuo in an attempt to appease the rebels, without effect. His commander Zhou Yafu cut the supply lines of the Wu and Chu forces, which collapsed from starvation. After the war the princes lost the right to appoint ministers for their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>, which strengthened central power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Succession was troubled. He made his eldest son Liu Rong crown prince in 153 BCE but deposed him in 150 BCE after the intrigues of his sister Princess Liu Piao, and Liu Che, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>, became crown prince. In 143 BCE Zhou Yafu was accused of what the interrogator called underground treason and killed himself in prison, an incident for which Emperor Jing was much criticised. He died in 141 BCE and was buried in the Han Yang Ling Mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>.</p><p>The joint reign of Wen and Jing, known as the Rule of Wen and Jing, is regarded as one of the golden ages of Chinese history, although Jing lacked his father's warmth and openness. In 2016 the earliest known traces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> were announced from his mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Jing of Han” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was divided into commanderies administered by centrally appointed officials, and after the Rebellion of the Seven States in 154 BCE, <b>Emperors Jing</b> and Wu progressively reduced the autonomous kingdoms to centrally controlled units.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The title Tao Te Ching, which marked the work as a classic, was first applied under <b>Emperor Jing of Han</b> (157–141 BCE), though it appears not to have been widely used until near the end of the Han.</blockquote><a href="/work/tao-te-ching">Tao Te Ching · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=-158" aria-label="Taiyuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7357 802 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emperor Jing of Han"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=-158">What was here in 158 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
